921	      <p class="callout" style="margin-top:16px;">
922	        <strong>Always show a label alongside the spinner.</strong> "Saving…" is better than a spinner alone — it tells the user what is happening, not just that something is. Use the progressive form of the verb matching the button's action.
923	      </p>
924	      <p class="callout">
925	        <strong>Implementation pattern (React):</strong> Add <code>is-loading</code> class and the <code>disabled</code> attribute together when the async action fires. Remove both on resolution (success or error). Set a minimum spinner display time of ~300ms to avoid a flash for fast responses.
926	      </p>
927	      <p class="callout">
928	        <strong>Screen reader note:</strong> The <code>.btn-spinner</code> element should have <code>aria-hidden="true"</code> (as shown). The accessible name comes from the visible label text ("Saving…"). Optionally add <code>aria-live="polite"</code> on a status region to announce completion.
929	      </p>
930	    </section>
931	
932	
933	    <!-- ── Usage Rules ────────────────────────────────────────── -->
934	    <section class="section">
935	      <h2 class="section-title">Usage Rules</h2>
936	      <ul class="usage-list">
937	        <li><strong>Primary:</strong> One per view or modal. Use for the single highest-confidence action (Save, Submit, Confirm). Do not use more than one primary button in the same action group.</li>
938	        <li><strong>Secondary:</strong> Supporting actions that are readily available but not the main path (Edit, Manage, Add). Can appear in multiples.</li>
939	        <li><strong>Tertiary:</strong> Low-emphasis utility actions (Cancel, Go Back, View History). Never use for destructive actions.</li>
940	        <li><strong>Destructive (solid):</strong> Hard-delete and irreversible actions only (Delete, Remove, Revoke). Must always be paired with a confirmation step. Do not use for actions that can be undone.</li>
941	        <li><strong>Destructive (ghost icon):</strong> Prefer the ghost icon variant (<code>.btn-icon-destructive</code>) for table-row and list-item delete actions. The solid button is appropriate only in dedicated confirmation dialogs or isolated danger zones — not scattered across lists where it creates visual alarm at scale.</li>
942	        <li><strong>Icon buttons:</strong> Always require <code>aria-label</code>. Use the framework tooltip component (not the HTML <code>title</code> attribute) so the label surfaces on keyboard focus as well as hover.</li>
943	        <li><strong>Disabled:</strong> Only disable a button when you can communicate why nearby — e.g. "Complete required fields above." Never silently disable without context.</li>
944	        <li><strong>Focus ring:</strong> Implemented with <code>:focus-visible</code> — appears only during keyboard navigation, not on mouse click. Never suppress with <code>outline: none</code>.</li>
945	        <li><strong>Windows High Contrast mode:</strong> The <code>outline</code>-based focus ring will be overridden automatically by the OS in forced-colors mode. This is correct and expected behavior — do not compensate for it.</li>
946	      </ul>
947	
948	      <h3 style="font-size:14px; font-weight:700; margin: 32px 0 8px; color:#6b6459;">Patterns Not Yet Specced</h3>
949	      <ul class="usage-list" style="color:#6b6459;">
950	        <li><strong>Split button:</strong> A primary action button with an attached dropdown for variants (e.g. arXiv Check's "Save and… / Edit Next" toolbar). Adds significant complexity — worth a dedicated pattern when needed.</li>
951	        <li><strong>Danger confirmation pattern:</strong> The inline expand-to-confirm UX (click Delete → row reveals "Are you sure? [Confirm] [Cancel]") that pairs with the destructive button. Needs a separate interaction pattern spec.</li>
952	      </ul>
953	    </section>
